     1	# Using a Tessera V1 receipt: a guide for users
     2	
     3	> **OWNER DRAFT 2026-10-19, revised after Codex review (docs/reviews/exit-pages/); row 22 repairs 1–4 and the bootstrap caveat applied 2026-10-23 (docs/reviews/e9-packet/AUTHOR-RULINGS.md).** (Claude Opus 5.5, owner instance.) The E12 page of the
     4	> 30 Oct exit (A9.2, `docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md:208`; registered by
     5	> `docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-3.md:89-95`). It is based on `docs/V1-CONTRACT.md` (owner draft). It is written in
     6	> the plain style of `docs/plain/STE-STYLE.md`. It adds no rule. If it differs from A9, A10, the V1 profile or the
     7	> contract, they apply and this page is wrong. Markers such as [R4] point to the references at the end. The 4 Dec
     8	> relying-party test will test this page; that test has not occurred [R27].
     9	
    10	## What Tessera can establish, and what it does not establish
    11	
    12	This list is copied without change from the 2026-07-28 ruling [R1]. Its words "package", "payload" and "issuer"
    13	are the ruling's words.
    14	
    15	> Tessera can establish:
    16	> "This valid package reconstructs the same framed bytes recorded
    17	> at issuance."
    18	>
    19	> Tessera does not establish:
    20	> "The payload is true."
    21	> "The issuer understood it."
    22	> "The relying party interpreted it correctly."
    23	> "This was the package the relying party expected."
    24	> "The external thing named by the payload is authentic."
    25	
    26	## What a VALID V1 receipt tells you
    27	
    28	A receipt is the set of files that the service gives back for one content. The service is the Tessera V1
    29	program that makes receipts. A verifier is the program that checks a receipt.
    30	
    31	Some technical names appear in this guide. A key is a secret number that makes signatures. A signature is data that
    32	only the holder of a key can make. Anyone can check a signature with the public part of the key. SHA-256 is a hash:
    33	it turns any bytes into a short code. V1 assumes that nobody can find two different inputs with the same code; the
    34	checks below depend on that assumption [R2].
    35	
    36	DNS is the internet's name system. DNSSEC adds signatures to DNS records. Git is a system that saves versions of
    37	files. A git commit is one saved version. Bitcoin is a public chain of blocks, and each block states a time.
    38	OpenTimestamps records a hash in a Bitcoin block. A frame is the exact bytes that one service key signs. It fixes
    39	the content and the receipt's common information.
    40	
    41	If the verifier says VALID, these statements are true, under the conditions in "Conditions that V1 does not
    42	check" below [R2]. Statements 2, 3 and 4 also need the authority-record condition in that section [R2a]:
    43	
    44	1. The receipt fixes these exact content bytes. Under the registered cryptographic assumptions, a change of one bit
    45	   makes the check fail [R3].
    46	2. Someone gave these bytes to the Tessera V1 service on some occasion [R3].
    47	3. Both service keys signed frames that fix these bytes. The keys do not sign the raw content directly. A missing or
    48	   changed receipt signature makes the check fail. The receipt signatures are the two that the receipt's anchor
    49	   (`A_sig`) fixes. A possession signature is different: it shows that a key holder signed the key list, and another
    50	   valid possession signature over the same list can replace it without changing the verdict [R3].
    51	4. Two authority records named the two keys for `wamason.com`. One record is in DNS. The other is in a signed git
    52	   commit that Bitcoin recorded at least 6 blocks before the receipt [R4].
    53	5. The bytes and both signatures existed before a specific Bitcoin block. The verifier reports that block's
    54	   chain time as T [R4].
    55	6. The issue time that the service wrote is not before the start of the keys' window. T is not after the end of
    56	   the window. The verifier makes no other comparison with the window. These two checks do not show that the signing
    57	   happened inside the window [R5].
    58	
    59	T is a chain time. A chain time is the time that a Bitcoin block header states. It is not a measurement of real
    60	time [R6].
    61	
    62	## What a VALID receipt does not tell you
    63	
    64	A VALID verdict does not establish any of these [R7]:
    65	
    66	- who gave the bytes to the service;
    67	- what the bytes mean, or that they are true;
    68	- that a key was not revoked (the report says `revocation: NOT_EVALUATED`);
    69	- that a signature algorithm or key was still safe when it signed (`historical_security: NOT_EVALUATED`; see
    70	  "Historical security" below);
    71	- that the bytes existed after any time, or before other bytes;
    72	- any standing (the report says `standing: ABSENT/NOT_ISSUED_IN_PROFILE`);
    73	- that the two keys have different controllers. One controller holds both keys;
    74	- that the authority records were public.
    75	
    76	**Historical security.** To assess historical security, obtain authenticated safe-before cutoffs for each algorithm
    77	and key. Compare them with T, using the registered chain-time uncertainty. Assess the theft of a key separately.
    78	A comparison of T with a cutoff alone does not show that an algorithm was safe [R28].
    79	
    80	**Real time.** The verifier can also report a value U in a separate field, `utc_assessment`. U is a real-time
    81	upper bound only under an assumption, A-UTC. The verifier does not check A-UTC [R8]. A-UTC says this: at least one
    82	of the selected Bitcoin block and its five successors states a chain time no more than two hours before the time
    83	when the block was made [R9].
    84	
    85	Use U only with a VALID receipt and A-UTC for the selected Bitcoin block and its five successors. A U beside
    86	INVALID or CANNOT VERIFY does not show a valid service receipt [R8]. Always read U with A-UTC beside it.
    87	
    88	**More than one receipt.** The same content can have more than one VALID receipt, each with its own T. Identify a
    89	receipt by the SHA-256 hash of its `A_sig` bytes, not by the hash of its files [R10]. `A_sig` is the bytes that
    90	hold both frames and their signatures.
    91	
    92	## Conditions that V1 does not check
    93	
    94	Each statement in "What a VALID V1 receipt tells you" is true only if these conditions are true [R2]:
    95	
    96	- Nobody misused the signing capability of the service.
    97	- The authority records named the service's actual keys [R2a].
    98	- The registered signature and git-link security assumptions hold [R2a].
    99	- SHA-256 and OpenTimestamps are not broken.
   100	- The Bitcoin chain has no reorganization of 6 or more blocks [R11].
   101	- Your verifier release is authentic.
   102	
   103	**Capture of the authority records.** An attacker can get control of both the DNS record and the git repository.
   104	Then the attacker can make new receipts under its own keys, and **the verifier says VALID for them** [R12]. V1 has
   105	no protection against this: the service-key listing was removed from V1 [R13]. The verifier cannot see this
   106	capture. V1 also does not check that the records were public [R14].
   107	
   108	Receipts that the honest service signed stay protected in that case. This holds only while the service's own keys
   109	stay secret and nobody misuses them. The attacker cannot move their signatures to other keys or other bytes [R12].
   110	
   111	**Capture of the service's AWS account.** An attacker with the AWS account can sign with both keys. Its receipts
   112	are VALID. V1 gives no protection against this [R15].
   113	
   114	## Before you check a receipt
   115	
   116	1. Get the verifier release from its publisher.
   117	2. Check the release signature with an independently authenticated production release key [R16][R17]. **This step
   118	   is not usable yet.** The production release key and the channels that publish its fingerprint are not yet fixed
   119	   (see "Not yet fixed" below). Until they are, no release is for a real decision.
   120	3. Reject TEST keys. A release that is marked TEST is never for a real decision [R17].
   121	4. Do not trust a key that came with the release. A substituted release brings its own key [R17].
   122	5. Get all files of the receipt from the holder. The holder is the person who keeps a receipt.
   123	6. Do not reformat the receipt files. A changed indentation makes the verdict INVALID [R18].
   124	
   125	## How to read the verdict
   126	
   127	The verifier gives one of three verdicts, with a reason code [R19]:
   128	
   129	| Verdict | Meaning | What you do |
   130	|---|---|---|
   131	| VALID (`VALID_STRICT`) | All V1 checks passed. | Rely only on the statements in "What a VALID V1 receipt tells you". |
   132	| INVALID | A check failed. | Reject the receipt. INVALID does not show that the content is false. |
   133	| CANNOT VERIFY (`UNVERIFIABLE`) | The verifier does not have what it needs, or the receipt is outside V1. | Do not rely on it as a verified V1 receipt. CANNOT VERIFY does not show fraud, and it does not show that the content is false. If files are missing, ask the holder for them. |
   134	
   135	Three special rules apply [R20]:
   136	
   137	- The verifier can report a T with INVALID. That T is not evidence of a valid receipt.
   138	- `INVALID / ISSUANCE_WINDOW` can come from a later OpenTimestamps proof. Ask the holder for the service's
   139	  original proof.
   140	- The verifier cannot check that the git commit was public.
   141	
   142	## Decisions for each registered case
   143	
   144	The registered mapping lists these cases [R21]. For each case, the verifier uses the strict check only.
   145	
   146	The verdicts in the table are for the registered test files. Case b keeps the honest DNS and git evidence. Case d3
   147	keeps the honest DNS evidence and has no git evidence. Case d1 uses honest receipt a. A different file can give a
   148	different verdict [R21a].
   149	
   150	| Case | Situation | Verdict | Your decision |
   151	|---|---|---|---|
   152	| a, g | An honest receipt, as the service shipped it. | VALID | Accept only this, under the conditions above: these bytes were given to the service, and they existed no later than chain time T. |
   153	| b | An attacker signs the content again with its own keys. The honest DNS and git evidence stays. | INVALID | Reject. |
   154	| c | Someone puts a receipt inside a second layer. | CANNOT VERIFY | Reject. |
   155	| d1 | Someone asks for a check with fewer checks than strict, then asks for the strict check of honest receipt a. | The verifier refuses the first request. The strict check gives VALID. | Accept only the result of the strict check. The refused request does not become VALID. |
   156	| d2 | An honest receipt without its git evidence. | CANNOT VERIFY | Reject. This is an honest receipt that V1 rejects. Ask the holder for the complete receipt. |
   157	| d3 | A copy with one attacker key in both slots. It has the honest DNS evidence and no git evidence. | INVALID | Reject. |
   158	| e, e′ | One key was revoked, before or after the issue time. | VALID | The receipt does not show that the key was not revoked. If your decision needs that, get other evidence. |
   159	| f | Another person sends content through the honest service. | VALID | The receipt does not show who sent the content. If your decision needs the sender, check the sender yourself. |
   160	| h | An honest receipt inside a renewal layer. | CANNOT VERIFY | Reject. This is an honest receipt that V1 rejects. |
   161	| i | The content is a contract with two valid signatures of the parties. | VALID | Accept only this: the signed contract existed before the Bitcoin block whose chain time is T. T is not a real-time bound [OWNER 2026-10-19, PR-01]. You must check the parties' signatures and keys yourself. |
   162	| i′ | The same, but one party signature does not verify. | VALID | Reject the contract. The receipt is valid, but your own check of a party signature fails. The service does not read the content. |
   163	
   164	Cases e, f and i′ have one hazard. The verdict is VALID, but the receipt does not answer your question [R22].
   165	
   166	## Open items in this draft
   167	
   168	- The release key and its fingerprint are not yet fixed. A TEST release key must never appear on this page [R23].
   169	- The scenario wording follows the registered mapping. A10 narrowed T to chain time and the window to two
